# CMS / i18n Conventions

## No hardcoded user-facing text

Do **not** put literal **copy** (titles, labels, buttons, descriptions, worded placeholders, etc.) in TSX or TS. All translatable user-facing text must come from **next-intl message files**.

Before adding keys, confirm where messages live in this project (commonly `web/data/languages/<locale>.json`). Read an existing locale file to match its structure.

- **Usage:** `getTranslations` (server) or `useTranslations` (client) with a namespace; reference message keys for every visible string.

**Translation variable naming:** Use `t` for the primary namespace. When you also use a shared namespace (e.g. `Common`), name that translator `commonT`.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — hardcoded copy
<h1>Learn to trade for free</h1>
<button>Sign in</button>

// ✅ GOOD
const t = useTranslations("Landing.hero");
<h1>{t("title")} <span>{t("titleAccent")}</span></h1>

const t = useTranslations("Navbar");
<button>{t("login")}</button>

// ✅ GOOD — shared namespace
const t = useTranslations("Navbar");
const commonT = useTranslations("Common");
<span>{commonT("logo")}</span>
```

## Language-independent literals

Strings that **do not change with locale** may be hardcoded in TSX/TS. Do **not** add a `global.json` (or similar) for these — that would be another message file for values a translator never touches. Shared *translated* chrome still belongs in the `Common` namespace of each locale file.

Hardcode when the visible value is the same in every language:

- Glyph-only placeholders (`••••••••`)
- Bare numerals and numeric decoration (`38`, `/100`)
- Initials fallbacks (`SS`), punctuation, symbols, separators that are not words

If a translator would rewrite it (including “100 / 100”, “Show password”, or a worded placeholder like `you@example.com`), it is copy — put it in a locale file.

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — same in every locale
<input type="password" placeholder="••••••••" />
<p>/{max}</p>
return `${parts[0][0]}${parts[1][0]}`.toUpperCase();

// ❌ BAD — words or locale-specific phrasing
<input placeholder="Enter your password" />
<p>{score} out of {max}</p>
```

## Key structure (PAGE_SECTION_ELEMENT)

Use **nested keys** by page/feature. Namespace = page or feature (e.g. `Landing`, `Auth`, `Navbar`). Under that: section and element (e.g. `hero.title`, `LoginModal.emailPlaceholder`).

- **Namespace**: page or feature — `Landing`, `Auth`, `Navbar`, `Footer`, `Metadata`
- **Section**: area or component — `hero`, `features`, `LoginModal`
- **Element**: type or role — `title`, `subtitle`, `cta`, `label`, `placeholder`

Example keys: `Landing.hero.title`, `Auth.LoginModal.emailPlaceholder`, `Footer.copyright`.

## When adding or changing UI text

1. Add or update the key in **every** locale file the app ships.
2. Use the **same key path** in each file so locales stay in sync.
3. In code, use `useTranslations("Namespace")` or `useTranslations("Namespace.section")` and `t("key")` or `t("section.key")`.

## Interpolation and rich values

- For dynamic values (e.g. year in copyright), use ICU-style placeholders: `"copyright": "© {year} Company. All rights reserved."` and call `t("copyright", { year: new Date().getFullYear() })`.
- For arrays (e.g. feature lists), store the array in JSON and use `t.raw("key")`.

## Optional validation

A script or CI step can validate that every key in one locale file exists in all others.

## CMS tree (`cms` property)

**Only when the project includes a config-driven CMS renderer** (e.g. `CmsTree` in `web/utils/cms.tsx` — verify the path and API in the repo before use).

For long-form copy driven by JSON, the namespace may include a **`cms`** object: nested keys are **slot strings**; **string values** are literal translated text per locale (not references to other keys).

### Slot syntax

- **Do not use `.` in JSON keys** — next-intl reserves `.` for nesting.
- Format: **`tag`** or **`tag_class1_class2`** (underscores between tag and classes).
- Examples: `"div_container"` → `<div className="container">…</div>`; `"h1_text-center"` → `<h1 className="text-center">…</h1>`.

### Nesting

- **Object value** → children rendered inside that element.
- **String value** → leaf text for that locale.

### Repeated siblings

Object keys must be unique. For several paragraphs or wrappers, use an **array of branch objects**.

### Example

```json
{
  "Disclaimer": {
    "cms": {
      "div_container": {
        "h1_title": "Limitation of Liability",
        "p_body-text": "The information provided by..."
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Reuse the **same keys** in every locale file; only string values differ.

```tsx
<CmsTree translation="Disclaimer" />
```

### Security (when CMS renderer exists)

- Only **whitelisted tags** (check `CMS_TEXT_BLOCK_TAGS` or equivalent in the project's CMS module). No `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.

### Server vs client (when CMS renderer exists)

- Client: `<CmsTree translation="Namespace" />`
- Server RSC: `<CmsTreeServer translation="Namespace" />` — use whichever the project exports.
